\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs32 \cf0 My plan wasn\'92t to start a business. I\'92d moved to Delhi and it was my chance to \'93give back\'94. I was going to train as a yoga instructor (I\'92d always hated yoga, but it felt right) and volunteer for charity (I had the time and felt disenchanted with London grind). I volunteered at a soup kitchen for half a day and weeped with stingy eyes as I chopped onions very slowly. I taught some English lessons where I realised teaching really is a skill - and one I didn\'92t yet have. Very quickly it became clear that the people running these centres were the ones who should be doing the work on the ground. Not me. They really didn\'92t need my time, in fact it was a burden. But, they really did need my money. Pounds go an awfully long way in rupees.\'a0
\f1\fs24 \

\f2\fs32 So, I started freelancing for the highest day rates I could muster and giving as much as I felt I could afford away.\'a0
\f1\fs24 \

\f2\fs32 Then work started building. I started hiring - and it got harder to simply transfer something every month.\'a0
\f1\fs24 \
\

\f2\fs32 I wanted to make a commitment that I could sustain. Always. Not just in months where there was a big windfall, but even when the going got tough. I remember in GSCE Religious Education learning about a tithe, where 10% of your earnings would go to charity and thinking that made so much sense. What would be my equivalent?
\f1\fs24 \
\

\f2\fs32 I\'92d read about\'a0 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.google.com/search?cs=0&sca_esv=76b1d1510d0b1bcc&sxsrf=AE3TifMfEv-bavg4GydGdXXUmaVlNmiskw%3A1760450899178&q=%221%25+for+the+Planet%22&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwjiys3T7qOQAxUkQUEAHZEoNN4QxccNegQIAhAC&mstk=AUtExfAYYbe5t_u9Q6PWrJc2qqObp1xrj12O9MaWb1c6YS2oFveipUT_4xdrCHJTXyTCCok6d5mPWXc08pIHRY1hhCA0_sKNGpi-igXUKmiEx9swFpkV5dCIVWsT6sD4kyowRKcK6fHt7pF06TsJPMK1kp7hJs2aAfzwAtlFwhfiQt_BIx4&csui=3"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \cb4 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 "1% for the Planet"}}\cf5 \cb4 \strokec5  (when businesses commit to giving 1% away) and it always struck me as pitiful. 1%?! That\'92s honestly nothing. As I started to work out my P&L I realised that committing to even a tiny bit adds pressure. I was working with Elizabeth Scarlett and was really inspired by CEO Alex and Founder Elizabeth\'92s principles - they would give 2% of their revenue to support animal conversation causes forever.\'a0
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \cb1 \strokec2 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs32 \cf5 \cb4 \strokec5 I settled on 2%. More than 1% but not more than I could afford. Crucially, it\'92s 2% of REVENUE, not profit, so I can\'92t give myself a huge payout to reduce the amount left to give.\'a0
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \cb1 \strokec2 \

\f2\fs32 \cf5 \cb4 \strokec5 I committed to myself, to my team then and to every team member in the future, that it would never waver. We will make every tough decision we have to before we compromise on the donation.\'a0
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \cb1 \strokec2 \
\

\f2\fs32 \cf5 \cb4 \strokec5 To date we\'92ve given over \'a3100,000. I am incredibly proud of it.\'a0
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \cb1 \strokec2 \
\

\f2\fs32 \cf5 \cb4 \strokec5 We support charities that help extremely socially and economically disadvantaged women. For 5 years we\'92ve fully funded the Laadlhi Boarding House in Jodhpur, Rajasthan. There 19 girls aged 6-14 are housed, fed and go to school. The girls aren\'92t orphaned, but their families can\'92t afford to look after them, so they have a safe, productive space to learn and play and can visit their families in the arid deserts around Jodphur. I visited in 2022 and every year one of my team goes there to see their work first hand.\'a0
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \cb1 \strokec2 \
\

\f2\fs32 \cf5 \cb4 \strokec5 When the amount we had to donate rose, we added a second charity, closer to home. Luminary Bakery works with victims of domestic violence in Lond, teaching them to bake so they can become financially independent and rebuild confidence.\'a0
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \cb1 \strokec2 \
\

\f2\fs32 \cf5 \cb4 \strokec5 It will be a very happy day, soon, when we start a new project to find our third partner.\'a0
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \cb1 \strokec2 \
\

\f2\fs32 \cf5 \cb4 \strokec5 This Christmas we want to fill LinkedIn with something wholesome. We want to celebrate the incredible brands in our community. Our 2% commitment aims to tackle a capitalist problem - that everything is about value creation, and not about distribution.\'a0
\f1\fs24 \cf0 \cb1 \strokec2 \
\

